WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.470
Last night my phone said, “Someone is

2
00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:05.390
trying to sign in to your email.”

3
00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:07.270
They had my password.

4
00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:09.490
But they still couldn’t get in.

5
00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:10.570
Why not?

6
00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.900
Today: two-step sign-in,

7
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.420
passkeys, and why a stolen

8
00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:18.920
password alone is no longer enough.

9
00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.120
Two-step sign-in asks for

10
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:23.200
two kinds of proof.

11
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.280
Something you know, like your password.

12
00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.260
Something you have, like your phone.

13
00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:32.030
Or something you are, like

14
00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:32.590
your fingerprint.

15
00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.680
A thief with your password still doesn’t

16
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:37.840
have your phone, so they’re stuck.

17
00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.380
Not all second steps are equal.

18
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.120
A code by text message is better

19
00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:46.240
than nothing, but a criminal can

20
00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:48.140
trick your phone company into moving

21
00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:49.420
your number to their SIM.

22
00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:52.520
An authenticator app makes a new code

23
00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:54.920
every thirty seconds, right on your phone.

24
00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:57.790
Strongest of all are passkeys.

25
00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.290
A passkey lets you sign in with your

26
00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:04.010
phone’s fingerprint, face or screen lock.

27
00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:06.530
There’s no password to type, and

28
00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:08.050
nothing a scammer can copy.

29
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.180
And a passkey only works on the real

30
00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.020
website, so a fake page gets nothing.

31
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.820
Google, Microsoft, Apple and WhatsApp

32
00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.420
accounts already offer them.

33
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:21.060
Quick check.

34
00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.310
My phone asks, “Are you

35
00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:24.350
trying to sign in?”

36
00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:26.760
But I’m not signing in to anything.

37
00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:28.700
What do I do?

38
00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:32.270
Deny it, then change your password,

39
00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:34.230
because someone out there has it.

40
00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:36.940
Never approve a prompt you didn’t

41
00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:39.060
start, however many times it asks.

42
00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:42.660
Some attackers send prompt after prompt,

43
00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.320
hoping you’ll tap yes just to make it

44
00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:45.580
stop.

45
00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:47.780
Where do you start?

46
00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.380
With your email, because it can

47
00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:51.820
reset every other account.

48
00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:55.190
Then WhatsApp: turn on two-step

49
00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:58.230
verification, and choose a six-digit PIN.

50
00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:00.970
Then your mobile money, your school

51
00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:03.450
account and anything with pupils’ data.

52
00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:05.860
What if your phone is lost?

53
00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.700
When you turn on two-step sign-in,

54
00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:10.420
you’re given backup codes.

55
00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.010
Write them down on paper, and keep

56
00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:14.810
them at home, not in your phone.

57
00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:16.720
They’re your spare key.

58
00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:19.540
Two warning signs to know.

59
00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:22.690
Anyone who asks for your code is a

60
00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:24.550
scammer, even if they say they’re

61
00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:26.030
the bank, or a friend.

62
00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:29.270
And if your phone suddenly shows “No

63
00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:31.910
service” for no reason, call your

64
00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:34.390
phone company at once: someone may

65
00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:35.310
be moving your number.

66
00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:37.370
Your turn.

67
00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:40.330
A colleague can choose a text message

68
00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:44.070
code, an authenticator app or a passkey.

69
00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:45.740
Which is strongest?

70
00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:49.730
A passkey: it can’t be phished,

71
00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:51.270
and there’s no code to steal.

72
00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:55.420
Remember: two kinds of proof, deny prompts

73
00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.780
you didn’t start, start with your email,

74
00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:00.320
and keep your backup codes on paper.

75
00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:02.830
Try it in class tomorrow:

76
00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:04.590
play “Two locks”.

77
00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:07.590
One pupil plays a thief who knows the

78
00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:10.030
password, and the class decides which

79
00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:12.810
second step stops the thief, and why.
